Here's the formal **testsmells.org 19-smell academic audit** for your integration tests:

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 0           | 0              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **3**       | **3**          |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Smell #3: Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // blocks 3 seconds, non-deterministic
```

**Why it matters (even for integration tests):** `Thread.Sleep` is never a reliable synchronization mechanism. Under load or CI, 3 seconds may not be enough; locally it wastes time.

**Fix:** Poll with a timeout or use an event/semaphore:
```csharp
var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id) && sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    await Task.Delay(100);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Smell #1: Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why it matters:** Only one branch executes per run. If the code changes which type is returned, the other assertion path is silently skipped — a hidden testing gap.

**Fix:** Write two tests with explicit expectations:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    // arrange a standard user
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<User>(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasDiscount()
{
    // arrange a premium user
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("premium@example.com");
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Smell #4: Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` · **Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Why it matters:** This will pass even if every insert silently fails (e.g., rows are discarded). It gives false confidence in coverage.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining 5 tests (`InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`, `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord`, `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`, `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted`, `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`) are well-structured:

- Clear Arrange / Act / Assert separation
- Targeted assertions on meaningful properties
- Proper use of `[TestCategory("Integration")]` with in-memory SQLite (hermetic — no Mystery Guest penalty)
- Count assertions immediately after inserting known items (not magic numbers)

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Impact |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free (`BulkInsert`) | ~2 min | Eliminates a false-pass risk |
| 2 | Conditional Logic (`GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType`) | ~10 min | Removes hidden gap |
| 3 | Sleepy Test (`NotifyOnInsert`) | ~15 min | Removes flakiness & speeds CI |

**Bottom line:** 5 of 8 tests are solid. The 3 high-severity findings are all straightforward to fix and would meaningfully improve reliability.
